.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt HANDLES 7
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm handles
.Nd handles, rights, and the authority of a session
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On ABSD a process can do exactly what the handles it holds allow, plus
starting programs if it holds the spawn right.
There are no users, no permission bits, and no ambient access to devices
or files.
.Ss Handles
A handle is an opaque value, private to one process, that names one kernel
object: a pipe, a process, a cell (a small versioned value), the console,
the block device, or the network card.
The kernel looks a handle up only in the calling process's own table; a
stale, guessed, or wrong-kind value is refused
.Pq error \-9 .
.Ss Rights
Each handle carries rights: read, write, wait, and kill.
An operation that needs a right the handle lacks is refused
.Pq error \-1 .
Rights only narrow: a process can make a second handle to the same object
with fewer rights, never more, and a handle copied into a child at start
never has more rights than the parent's.
.Pp
Handles move only when a process is started, and always as copies.
There is no passing of handles over pipes, and process handles are never
given away: only the parent holds one for its child.
.Pp
The
.Em spawn right
is not a handle right but an attribute of the process.
A child inherits it unless its parent withholds it, and nothing restores
it.
Without it a process cannot start anything.
.Pp
Parentage, adoption by init, a PID, or a restart never grants a right.
.Ss Files are not handles
A file is not a kernel object.
A process reaches files through a file server channel, and the server keeps
that channel's mount list; see
.Xr filesystem 7 .
A process may have the server bind a spare channel for a child with its own
mounts or fewer, never more.
.Ss The authority of a session
The SSH server gives a session's first process (the shell, or an
.Ql ssh host command
command) exactly:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
three pipes: standard input, output, and error;
.It
one file server channel bound to
.Pa /home/operator
and
.Pa /tmp/session-N
read-write and
.Pa /bin ,
.Pa /etc ,
.Pa /share
read-only, and, for a shell, up to two spare channels for its commands;
.It
the working directory
.Pa /
and an empty environment;
.It
the spawn right, only if it is the shell (the shell's own commands never
get it);
.It
for a shell only, a session control channel: two pipes to the SSH server,
one on which the server tells the shell of ^C and of the session's end and
answers it, one on which the shell asks for
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr shutdown 8 ,
or
.Xr reboot 8 .
The shell never gives it to a command.
.El
.Pp
It gets no console, no network card, no block device handle of any kind,
no machine handle, and no way to reach
.Pa /ssh .
Stopping or restarting the machine takes a machine handle, which only init
holds; a session asks the SSH server, which ends its sessions and exits,
and init decides.
.Pp
This is the ceiling.
The shell's authority is derived from it, and a command's from the
shell's, and each step may only narrow: a command gets the shell's mounts,
the same read-only
.Pq Ic ro ,
or none
.Pq Ic nofs ;
see
.Xr absd-sh 1 .
A command whose output is redirected to a file holds a pipe, not the file,
and gains no authority over that file or any other.
Commands run directly by
.Ql ssh host command
cannot start anything.
.Pp
When the session ends its channels are released, and anything it left
running loses its filesystem authority.
A request already on a channel when it is released is still read, and
refused; a later binding of the channel discards what predates it.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr absd-sh 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7 ,
.Xr process 7
.Pp
In the kernel repository:
.Pa docs/TERMINOLOGY.md
(Authority) and
.Pa docs/PROCESS-STARTUP.md .
.Sh CAVEATS
One operator: every session has the same ceiling and shares
.Pa /home/operator ;
the only files one session has that another cannot reach are in its
.Pa /tmp/session-N .
.Pp
A process holding a handle with no rights to a pipe can still learn how
full that pipe is.
